hl|THE HEADLINE — WHAT THE 3.1 KB KEY ACTUALLY CARRIES
hl|1|3,174 bytes reach 0.2070 forward x1 accuracy. 500,000 bytes reach 0.3523.
hl|1|the key is 0.63% of the bytes and delivers 58.8% of the predictive power.
hl|2|it covers 79.67% of held-out contexts from 3,174 bytes. coverage is the
hl|2|cheap part of language. accuracy inside coverage is the expensive part.
hl|3|locality shows up here too, independently of the compressor: the ADJACENT
hl|3|3,174 bytes beat the KEY 0.2245 to 0.2070. same size, same everything else.
hl|3|that is the third independent sighting of the locality premium.
hl|4|RANDOM scores 0.0000 on every column. this is WHY priorC317400 made the
hl|4|payload 13,085 B worse. noise is not a weak prior, it is a harmful one.

det|A CHEAP SCREEN THAT FELL OUT OF THIS — contexts per byte
dt|1|distinct frozen contexts / training bytes:
dt|1|KEY 508/3174 = 0.160   ADJACENT 557/3174 = 0.176
dt|1|RANDOM 3104/3174 = 0.978   BIG 4062/500000 = 0.008
dt|2|the RANDOM arm produces ALMOST ONE CONTEXT PER BYTE — nothing repeats.
dt|2|English collapses into few, frequent contexts. so context-count is
dt|2|ANTI-correlated with usefulness.
dt|3|therefore: a candidate prior can be screened in O(n), on the prior alone,
dt|3|with no corpus and no compressor run. high contexts-per-byte = it will hurt.
dt|3|this is a new test and it costs milliseconds. it should gate every prior
dt|3|before any 2-minute compressor arm is spent on it.

